1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,481
This links on really nicely to the sixth habit which is keeping a not to do list.
这就很自然地引出了第六个习惯：列一张“不做清单”。

2
00:00:04,481 --> 00:00:10,240
Top 1 women know that their time attention and energy is the most precious finite resource that they have.
她说，顶尖女性明白，时间、注意力和精力是自己最珍贵、也有限的资源。

3
00:00:10,240 --> 00:00:13,281
And this is the one habit that really changed things the most for me.
对我来说，这是最改变生活的一个习惯。

4
00:00:13,281 --> 00:00:18,401
For years I was used to leaving my time and energy open to anybody who wanted to access it.
多年来，只要有人需要，我就习惯于把自己的时间和精力向对方敞开。

5
00:00:18,401 --> 00:00:24,321
Any tasks or to dos or hangouts with people I would automatically default to saying yes to.
无论是任务、待办事项，还是和别人出去玩，我都会下意识地答应。

6
00:00:24,321 --> 00:00:27,921
But at some point you realize that your life is almost overflowing with things to do.
但到某个时候，你会发现，生活里要做的事情已经多得快要装不下了。

7
00:00:27,921 --> 00:00:35,521
And it's only by saying no to some things that we can make the space for truly needle moving activities that help to unblock a bottleneck.
只有拒绝一些事情，才能给真正推动进展、帮助突破瓶颈的活动留出空间。

8
00:00:35,521 --> 00:00:41,911
This can range all the way from small things such as in the morning on my not to do list is not to pick up the phone first thing in the morning.
小到这样的事情：我的“不做清单”里，有一条就是早上醒来第一件事不要拿手机。

9
00:00:41,921 --> 00:00:47,601
This is one that I sometimes struggle with because it's so easy to immediately as soon as you wake up have your brain chatter start.
这一点我有时也做不到，因为刚醒来，大脑就很容易开始叽叽喳喳地想个不停。

10
00:00:47,601 --> 00:00:54,240
And you're like oh I wonder what's happening in all the various different fronts of the business in the email inbox in the messages.
我就会想：业务的各个方面怎么样了？邮箱里有什么？消息里又有什么？

11
00:00:54,240 --> 00:01:00,641
It's so easy to think that and so one of my implementation intentions is actually not to pick up my phone first thing after I wake up.
很容易就会这么想，所以我的一个具体行动打算，就是醒来后先不拿手机。

12
00:01:00,641 --> 00:01:08,961
This can also range to the big things such as saying no to a trip or a holiday which you know won't actually take you towards your goals and you don't really want to go on.
大一点的事也一样，比如拒绝一次不能让你更接近目标、而且你也并不想去的旅行或假期。

13
00:01:08,961 --> 00:01:16,081
Another example of this is when let's say meetings overrun regularly and actually stopping allowing this to continually happen.
另一个例子是，假设会议经常超时，你要停止放任这种情况继续发生。

14
00:01:16,081 --> 00:01:21,440
And instead being like okay actually like we want to stop having meetings overrun how can we do this.
我们可以提出：好，我们想让会议不再超时，那要怎么做？

15
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:25,201
We can set a clear agenda we can time manage better throughout the meeting.
可以设定清晰的议程，并在整场会议中更好地管理时间。

16
00:01:25,201 --> 00:01:29,521
And then we can actually say you know what at the end of the meeting that's it like that's the hard stop.
然后明确说：到了会议结束时间，就到此为止，那是必须结束的时间点。

17
00:01:29,521 --> 00:01:32,800
If we don't manage to talk about everything then we'd have to just talk about it next time.
如果没能把所有事情都谈完，就得留到下次再谈。

18
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:36,800
And this practice actually trains us to work better with the time that we have.
这种做法其实是在训练我们，更好地利用现有的时间。

19
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:43,680
And this point on the not to do list not overrunning meetings all the time is actually so crucial because then it allows you to trust yourself more.
“不做清单”上不再让会议一直超时这一点非常关键，因为它能让你更加信任自己。

20
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:47,440
Frees up your time gives you more clarity on when your meetings are starting and ending.
它还会腾出时间，让你更清楚会议什么时候开始、什么时候结束——

21
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:52,081
Makes you more efficient in the meetings itself and also helps you and your team with time management.
让你开会时效率更高，也帮助你和团队管理时间。

22
00:01:52,081 --> 00:01:56,560
And one thing to mention here is that if you stop doing things or decide things on your not to do list.
还有一点：当你停做一些事情，或决定把一些事情列入“不做清单”时——

23
00:01:56,560 --> 00:02:02,800
It's quite common to have feelings of guilt or wondering like oh maybe like I shouldn't actually say no to this.
很常见的感受是内疚，或者会想：也许我不该拒绝这件事。

24
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:10,560
But it's an important question to ask yourself whether the current thing that you're doing and accepting in your life is going to take you towards living your dream life.
但有一个重要的问题值得问自己：现在正在做、正在接受进入生活的事情，是否会让你更接近想过的生活？

25
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:15,831
And if you ask that question about each moment in your day what answers come up for you.
如果你把一天中的每一刻都拿来问这个问题，会得到什么答案？

26
00:02:15,841 --> 00:02:18,800
Habit number seven is outsource before you're overwhelmed.
第七个习惯，是在自己忙到不堪重负之前就开始外包。

27
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:23,040
Top 1 women treat their attention and energy as their most valuable resource.
她说，顶尖女性把注意力和精力视为最宝贵的资源。

28
00:02:23,040 --> 00:02:26,721
Buying back their time then becomes their highest roi investment.
因此，对她们来说，花钱换回自己的时间就成了回报率最高的投资。

29
00:02:26,721 --> 00:02:31,361
What this often demands of us is delegating earlier than feels comfortable for us.
这往往要求我们，在还觉得不太自在的时候就更早地把事情分派出去。

30
00:02:31,361 --> 00:02:36,640
There may be things in your life which you already know how to do you don't particularly enjoy it doesn't have to be you.
生活中有些事情，你已经会做，却并不特别喜欢做，而且也并非非得由你来做。

31
00:02:36,640 --> 00:02:40,081
This could actually be done by somebody else and yet you're still doing it yourself.
这些事情其实可以由别人来做，但你依然自己做。

32
00:02:40,081 --> 00:02:42,881
If you're the only person working on your life or business.
如果你的生活或生意中，所有事情都只有你一个人处理——

33
00:02:42,881 --> 00:02:48,881
Then you will always be limited by how many hours of time and how much energy and attention you have in a day.
你就始终受限于一天拥有多少时间、精力和注意力。

34
00:02:48,881 --> 00:02:51,841
If you want to be able to scale beyond that you need to learn how to delegate.
如果想突破这个规模，你就需要学习分派任务。

35
00:02:51,841 --> 00:02:56,961
Some examples of this might be hiring a virtual assistant or a video editor if you're making videos.
例如，可以雇一位远程助理；如果你做视频，也可以雇一位视频剪辑师。

36
00:02:56,961 --> 00:03:00,081
Or a generalist who can help you across lots of different parts of your business.
或者雇一位能够帮你处理业务中多个不同部分的综合型人员。

37
00:03:00,081 --> 00:03:04,400
Or even hiring a nanny to help you at home and buy back some of your time there hiring a cleaner.
也可以请保姆帮忙照顾家里，从而腾出一些时间；或者请清洁人员。

38
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:11,111
One way to think about this is to think of your ideal hourly rate how much would it cost for somebody to buy an hour of your time.
一种思考方式是想想你理想中的时薪：别人要付多少钱，才能买下你一个小时的时间？

39
00:03:11,121 --> 00:03:17,751
Let's take the example of hiring a cleaner to come in once a week or something like that to help you with the usual tasks that you would do at home.
拿雇清洁人员每周来一次之类的安排为例，帮你处理平时在家会做的那些家务。

40
00:03:17,761 --> 00:03:21,681
If you think about the hours that you spend cleaning your home on a weekly basis.
想一想，你每周花多少小时打扫家里——

41
00:03:21,681 --> 00:03:25,121
And you also imagine the cost of hiring a cleaner to complete the same job for you.
再想一想，请清洁人员替你完成同样工作要花多少钱。

42
00:03:25,121 --> 00:03:29,041
Is it worth paying that amount of money to buy back those hours of your life.
花这笔钱换回生命中的这几个小时，值得吗？
